WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.230
A core metacognitive skill is knowing what you know, what you partly know,

00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:06.454
and what you do not know yet.

00:00:06.454 --> 00:00:08.996
This sounds simple, but it is often difficult.

00:00:08.996 --> 00:00:13.444
People can feel confident because a topic is familiar, because the explanation sounded clear,

00:00:13.444 --> 00:00:16.303
or because they have seen the same words before.

00:00:16.303 --> 00:00:20.434
That feeling can be useful, but it is not proof of usable understanding.

00:00:20.434 --> 00:00:22.022
Familiarity and mastery are different.

00:00:22.022 --> 00:00:23.611
Familiarity means something feels recognizable.

00:00:23.611 --> 00:00:26.153
Mastery means you can explain, retrieve, apply, compare,

00:00:26.153 --> 00:00:30.601
or adapt the idea when the source is not directly in front of you.

00:00:30.601 --> 00:00:34.413
A fluent explanation can hide weak comprehension if the person has not

00:00:34.413 --> 00:00:36.002
tested the idea through examples,

00:00:36.002 --> 00:00:36.955
application, or recall.

00:00:36.955 --> 00:00:40.132
Confidence can rise faster than competence when feedback is missing.

00:00:40.132 --> 00:00:42.991
Illusions of understanding are common in learning and work.

00:00:42.991 --> 00:00:46.804
A person may believe they understand a system because they can follow

00:00:46.804 --> 00:00:49.028
a diagram while someone else explains it.

00:00:49.028 --> 00:00:51.252
Later, when asked to troubleshoot the system,

00:00:51.252 --> 00:00:54.747
they may discover that the relationships were never secure in memory.

00:00:54.747 --> 00:00:56.335
The issue is not intelligence.

00:00:56.335 --> 00:00:58.877
The issue is that recognition felt like capability.

00:00:58.877 --> 00:01:01.737
To test understanding, ask the idea to do work.

00:01:01.737 --> 00:01:03.643
Explain it in your own words.

00:01:03.643 --> 00:01:05.549
Apply it to a new example.

00:01:05.549 --> 00:01:07.138
Retrieve the steps without looking.

00:01:07.138 --> 00:01:09.679
Compare two similar cases and explain the difference.

00:01:09.679 --> 00:01:11.586
Teach the idea to someone else.

00:01:11.586 --> 00:01:13.174
These tests make gaps visible,

00:01:13.174 --> 00:01:17.940
and visible gaps are useful because they tell you what to learn or practice next.
